00:00.120 --> 00:00.800
Hey friends.

00:00.800 --> 00:05.240
So now we can talk about a very special kind of indexes in databases.

00:05.240 --> 00:07.360
We have the Columnstore index.

00:07.360 --> 00:10.240
This topic is very confusing for a lot of developers.

00:10.240 --> 00:11.320
But now what are we gonna do?

00:11.360 --> 00:17.520
We're gonna deep dive on how SQL works with the Columnstore index, and why it is very important for

00:17.520 --> 00:19.200
big data and data analytics.

00:19.240 --> 00:22.720
And of course, we're going to go and practice by creating this index.

00:22.720 --> 00:23.440
So let's go.

00:27.440 --> 00:28.840
So now let's say that we have a table.

00:28.840 --> 00:31.080
We have multiple rows and multiple columns.

00:31.120 --> 00:35.240
Now if we use Rowstore index this is the classical one.

00:35.240 --> 00:36.160
What can happen.

00:36.200 --> 00:39.280
Our table is going to be split into multiple rows.

00:39.320 --> 00:43.320
And as we learned each group of rows is going to be stored inside a database.

00:43.440 --> 00:49.640
So that means we are organizing the data row by row, which means all the columns for each row are going

00:49.640 --> 00:51.160
to be stored together.

00:51.160 --> 00:57.640
This is the traditional way on how the databases organize their data, where the informations are stored

00:57.680 --> 00:58.640
row by row.

00:58.640 --> 01:03.680
But now in the other side, if you use Columnstore index, the SQL is going to go and split your table

01:03.680 --> 01:06.040
into multiple separate columns.

01:06.080 --> 01:11.400
And then SQL can go and store the values of one column together in database.

01:11.520 --> 01:16.160
So that means if you go and open a database, you will find only the values of one column.

01:16.160 --> 01:18.360
You will not find the entire row.

01:18.680 --> 01:22.320
So if it's like the first name, you will see only the first name informations.

01:22.320 --> 01:25.200
You will not see the last name information in this data page.

01:25.200 --> 01:31.160
So if you compare them the row store index store the data row by row that columnstore index stores the

01:31.160 --> 01:33.000
data column by column.

01:33.000 --> 01:37.760
So this is a very high level representation on how the Columnstore index is stored.

01:37.800 --> 01:43.960
As you know me, we go in details in order to understand exactly how SQL works with the Columnstore

01:43.960 --> 01:44.640
index.

